2e-b08c-8c068f5f6e49" :question/math-academy-id "q-79800" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)={x}^{2}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is not one of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-79800/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79800"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-79724"] :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}$\n- $y(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a function and the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-131013/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "163fd82e-824e-541e-ba58-607ebf4b9af4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131013/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "373737a2-fdd8-5002-90c4-e01010989661" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131013/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "a2ec5fcc-f4f0-5b60-abf7-66b387fc510d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131013/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "97a4c0b0-f3de-5b28-981e-2234e33e316b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131013/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "3747e8e6-b9e4-5038-8bc8-36a0ad39de46" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131013/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7bee083f-fb40-5275-a3fc-8147377d1d9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-131013/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-131013/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-131013/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-131013/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-131013/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-131013/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-131013" :question/id #uuid "a02f4b33-a5b8-5f4c-9305-d8176d9840e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-131013" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{4x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-12y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{{e}^{-3x}}$\n- $y(x)={e}^{4x}+{e}^{-3x}$\n- $y(x)={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{{e}^{-3x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{4x}+{e}^{-3x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{x}={e}^{4x}\\cdot {e}^{-3x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-131013/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-131013"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-79826/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "7cc7520d-9c43-5b9b-bd5c-1653a09ecacb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79826/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "982bc1a6-a598-50fc-bc6d-5312f838b32a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79826/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "c934f1f1-c922-5ad5-b317-553779acd642" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79826/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "79264ba9-31b4-5f55-b8ad-4a1e6217c468" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79826/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "00482635-bebb-5a06-a112-1e469ff33e86" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79826/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "27227c8e-ba56-58b3-bd9b-95f9fb9ddab7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-79826/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-79826/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-79826/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-79826/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-79826/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-79826/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79826" :question/id #uuid "f1c83c9e-49e0-5c44-a338-0361da3bd800" :question/math-academy-id "q-79826" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=x\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}$\n- $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=x\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a function and the known \nsolutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-79826/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79826"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-79830/selection/b1a9f2bb9f9bd59b66c906c9ab98446a26ec623744a7f92356bb121785f6cea5" :answer/id #uuid "0e319f75-aca9-5e1e-acb6-d495752c0034" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79830/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "9166bce4-3634-5e1c-8c3e-ed294047e766" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79830/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "68d90bc1-c928-59cf-8341-1112ed1a6d27" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79830/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "8d5fa24e-dc65-5911-9d3b-ed994e2aca76" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79830/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "1356f09f-12a1-537f-9506-c291fa199140" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79830/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "86058da3-cc50-5c8d-9dd0-c1823d49a53a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-79830/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-79830/selection/b1a9f2bb9f9bd59b66c906c9ab98446a26ec623744a7f92356bb121785f6cea5" "q-79830/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-79830/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-79830/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-79830/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79830" :question/id #uuid "902f8e05-10b0-58cd-8a55-2c894b53c82e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79830" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-4y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=3{x}^{4}$\n- $y(x)=-{x}^{2}$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=3{x}^{4}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $y(x)={x}^{4}$ is not a solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=-{x}^{2}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-1.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-79830/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79830"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-131022/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "0588687c-c693-584f-bc4c-3549d000d3d1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131022/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "c778259f-1272-5d91-8e86-459694de1c83" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131022/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "0756c3a4-35c8-5711-ad57-4579fc25c723" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131022/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "418ec1c5-491b-5b2b-be73-945bb782b374" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131022/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "e4017307-a832-54e6-bc2c-dc51aa3c6d3c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131022/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7911dc6f-02db-56ba-930a-f61f1029be93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-131022/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-131022/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-131022/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-131022/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-131022/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-131022/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-131022" :question/id #uuid "2081eb5b-57d7-57e6-a013-e3e8bcd2b543" :question/math-academy-id "q-131022" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{4}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-7x{y}^{′}+16y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=-2{x}^{4}$\n- $y(x)={x}^{4}\\sqrt{x}$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{e}^{x}}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=-2{x}^{4}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-2.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{4}\\sqrt{x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{e}^{x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotient of a known solution and a non-constant function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-131022/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-131022"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-131020/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "6413f2a0-6ea1-5c9a-8dc0-7f9c41840497" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131020/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "f0fdd4c9-acbe-5997-8ccc-436971a7c379" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131020/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "4c73d9b4-d20a-5dc8-a807-39dd4ca925fa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131020/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "f2e2e5ea-2b58-5561-b741-7c24c98a9f9c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131020/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "5ba70b58-63f8-529c-971d-f86a0e16f589" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131020/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "571a1259-2474-51be-819c-5bacde8bc540" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-131020/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-131020/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-131020/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-131020/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-131020/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-131020/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-131020" :question/id #uuid "51bd1b14-4e3d-59b8-aceb-0dbcc911c5cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-131020" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+9y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-3.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-131020/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-131020"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-79730"] :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(2x)$\n- $y(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(2x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-1.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-65112"] :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)=t$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}$ are solutions to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}-2t{y}^{′}+2y=0$ for $t>0,$ what function must also be a solution to the given differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(t)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(t)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(t)=t$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{2}\n$$\n\nIf we let ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we get:\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{t-{t}^{2}}{2}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-79799/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "161d0c77-dc99-53a8-ab98-7b8e03d93d86" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79799/selection/b1a9f2bb9f9bd59b66c906c9ab98446a26ec623744a7f92356bb121785f6cea5" :answer/id #uuid "5098e556-3303-5972-90ca-d7f8cc586183" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79799/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "f4fd9436-2955-56d8-86f3-8bbf62aabce2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79799/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "e8d8e4b5-ca78-56b1-8b37-ddcfbbcba552" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79799/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "72c418eb-a710-5b46-a94f-2f81ec420088" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79799/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b6e0db08-a413-5785-95bd-218eebe99cee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-79799/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-79799/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-79799/selection/b1a9f2bb9f9bd59b66c906c9ab98446a26ec623744a7f92356bb121785f6cea5" "q-79799/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-79799/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-79799/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79799" :question/id #uuid "742f0d7e-0be6-5200-afaf-b211266d03e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-79799" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{4t}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}-4y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(t)={e}^{-t-1}$\n- $y(t)=7{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-4t}$\n- $y(t)=\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{4}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(t)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(t)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{4t}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(t)={e}^{-t-1}=\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{e}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{e},{c}_{2}=0.$\n\n- The function $y(t)=7{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-4t}$ is **not** a solution of the given equation because ${e}^{-4t}$ is not a solution.\n\n- The function $y(t)=\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{4}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=0,{c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-79799/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79799"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-131029/selection/dede6d8db45d5f3b4afb226cedf13ee76190c94263d2c91589c3dc6c00983246" :answer/id #uuid "90572c68-9fcd-55ae-95f0-6e24f81b49f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={x}^{5}+2{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131029/selection/31017b989f52553e87c0bd5c2975a5de0e6738727901a2a58ab51679cb5503b7" :answer/id #uuid "92e14436-e144-5ede-9456-3da664822f83" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}+1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131029/selection/de5dead4f1c75fa4ac29c6da9ded71c519b139e5e890b233d1b4775798fbfa51" :answer/id #uuid "c5472481-624f-54f3-87e4-e5532015917d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{5}+2}{2{x}^{4}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131029/selection/b6a3891c06d30584b4e2fb4a68f5d36de39cfb3bd1ffdd6b6b08b26b5298862d" :answer/id #uuid "9f469a90-8161-544a-90e3-55196efe1ccf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{2{x}^{9}+3}{{x}^{4}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131029/selection/4025b4a65b43251e5a118093470e2cfb65d8dfc41db0dcef51204ad28c4064aa" :answer/id #uuid "59d0464b-2411-57a9-8ac9-4a7147906065" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={x}^{5}+2{x}^{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131029/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "016916d9-eaee-5c25-a2a8-06d1c47f0da5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-131029/selection/b6a3891c06d30584b4e2fb4a68f5d36de39cfb3bd1ffdd6b6b08b26b5298862d" :answer-field/choices ["q-131029/selection/dede6d8db45d5f3b4afb226cedf13ee76190c94263d2c91589c3dc6c00983246" "q-131029/selection/31017b989f52553e87c0bd5c2975a5de0e6738727901a2a58ab51679cb5503b7" "q-131029/selection/de5dead4f1c75fa4ac29c6da9ded71c519b139e5e890b233d1b4775798fbfa51" "q-131029/selection/b6a3891c06d30584b4e2fb4a68f5d36de39cfb3bd1ffdd6b6b08b26b5298862d" "q-131029/selection/4025b4a65b43251e5a118093470e2cfb65d8dfc41db0dcef51204ad28c4064aa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-131029" :question/id #uuid "93ee6e63-1b10-5325-b502-afabadb6eb8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-131029" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{5}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ are solutions to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-20y=0,$ what function must also be a solution to the given differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(x)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{5}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{5}+{c}_{2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\n$$\n\nIf we let ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=3,$ we get:\n\n$$\ny(x)=2{x}^{5}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}=\\frac{2{x}^{9}+3}{{x}^{4}}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-131029/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-131029"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-131036/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "bd6a854d-53d6-50fc-9107-0f2a59743d35" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131036/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "b10a246c-2b73-5575-81a7-4368685878df" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131036/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "72c5c4fe-f012-52cf-bde5-408c97208149" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131036/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "00c7d3ab-fb49-5d1b-ae29-52e25e1f95db" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131036/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "c80aa570-a716-5577-b61f-eb8cb7f8c12e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131036/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "02c77a78-348d-5138-8263-d15956b8585f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-131036/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-131036/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-131036/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-131036/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-131036/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-131036/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-131036" :question/id #uuid "f189b852-5b24-5d57-9057-b50373fc4db7" :question/math-academy-id "q-131036" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={e}^{x}+4{e}^{-x}$\n- $y(x)=5{e}^{-x}$\n- $y(x)=({e}^{-x}+1){e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(x)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{e}^{x}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{x}+4{e}^{-x}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=1,{c}_{2}=4.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=5{e}^{-x}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=0,{c}_{2}=5.$\n\n- The function $({e}^{-x}+1){e}^{x}=1+{e}^{x}$ is **not** a solution of the given equation because $1$ is not a solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-131036/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-131036"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
